<Base
  title="Gene explorer — JBrowseMSA"
  description="Pick a species and type a gene: a connected JBrowse session with a collapsed-intron genome view, a cross-species protein alignment, and the AlphaFold structure, synthesized in the browser from public data."
  wide
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Gene explorer</h1>
    <p>
      Pick a species — human, mouse, zebrafish, fly, worm, plant, or yeast — and
      type a gene name. You get a connected JBrowse session: the gene on the
      <strong>genome</strong> (introns collapsed so the coding exons sit side by side)
      and its <strong>AlphaFold 3D structure</strong>, with human genes also
      carrying a <strong>100-vertebrate protein alignment</strong>. Hover or
      select in any view and the others light up the matching residue —
      codon-precise, across genome, alignment, and structure. Everything is
      synthesized in the browser from public NCBI, UCSC GenArk, and AlphaFold
      data, with no per-gene files to host.
    </p>

    <GeneExplorer client:only="react" />
  </article>
</Base>
